    {
      "find": "I spend my days designing for healthcare platforms, booking systems, and other products where 'just figure it out' isn't an option. The people using these tools are busy, stressed, and have actual work to do. My job is to get out of their way.",
      "replace": "I spend my days on one question: how do you give someone the best part of a podcast without taking away the voice? Briefly is a short-form, audio-only podcast platform. It pulls the best moments out of full episodes and serves them in a feed tuned to your interests, the way Instagram and TikTok learn what you like."
    },
    {
      "find": "I've worked on teams of two and teams of twenty. I've had access to endless user research and I've had to make educated guesses with limited data. I've worked within strict component libraries and I've built design systems from scratch. What stays consistent is asking good questions, collaborating with people who know more than me, and shipping things that actually help",
      "replace": "Briefly is a team of three: me, a post-grad fellow formerly at Stanford HCI lab, and an engineer. I lead UI/UX, product design, and business strategy. We built the AI pipeline and recommendation engine together, and I direct coding agents and review their code, so I stay close to how an idea becomes a product."
    },
    {
      "find": "Currently, I'm at Meridian Health working on experiences for mental health clinicians and administrators. Before that, I spent almost two years at StyleBook helping salons manage their businesses (and discovering that stylists have very strong opinions about scheduling interfaces, which I deeply respect). ",
      "replace": "I started Briefly in May 2026 while studying Cognitive Brain Science and Computer Science at Tufts. We're mentored through the Tufts Derby Center for Entrepreneurship by a founder with multiple tech exits. The iOS app is launched on the App Store in October 2026, and fully supports CarPlay. "
    },
    {
      "find": "When I'm not designing, you'll find me thinking about why some interfaces feel intuitive and others make you want to throw your laptop out a window. Usually with coffee. ",
      "replace": "When I'm not working on Briefly, studying, I'm training Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu, cooking, spending time with family or friends, or practicing languages: English, Urdu, and Burmese (fluent), Chinese (intermediate), Turkish (beginner). "
    },
    {
      "find": "Starting with why, not what",
      "replace": "Start with the listener"
    },
    {
      "find": "Before jumping into wireframes, I want to understand what problem we're actually solving. Sometimes the thing people ask for isn't the thing they need. My favorite projects start with good questions and end with solutions that feel obvious in hindsight.",
      "replace": "Before I design a screen, I want to know when and where someone is listening: commuting, cooking, or in the car. The context decides everything else."
    },
    {
      "find": "think first, draw later.",
      "replace": "listen first, draw later."
    },
    {
      "find": "Designing for real constraints",
      "replace": "Design for real constraints"
    },
    {
      "find": "Perfect conditions don't exist. Budgets are tight, timelines are aggressive, legacy systems are messy, and sometimes you just can't talk to users directly. I'm comfortable making smart decisions with imperfect information and finding creative solutions within real limitations.",
      "replace": "A student-founded team of three has limited time and finances. I'd rather make a few things very good than many things half good."
    },
